<description>
Cycle each solid odd-sided square to the next smaller centered square, wrapping a single pixel to a 9x9 square.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- Every foreground object is a separated 4-connected monochrome solid square.
- Square side lengths are odd and belong to the cycle 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.

Rule:
- Clear the canvas to the background.
- Keep each square's color and center.
- Replace side length n by n - 2 when n > 1; replace side length 1 by side length 9.

Key insight:
- The visible operation is on square size, not color: side lengths cycle 1 -> 9 -> 7 -> 5 -> 3 -> 1.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "separated monochrome solid odd-sided square objects on the background",
  "transform": "clear each original square and repaint a centered square of the same color with side length n - 2 when n > 1; a 1x1 object wraps to a centered 9x9 square"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 16x16, background 7 [observed]
  objects: 3-5 monochrome solid squares, each a distinct non-bg color
  side_lengths: each from {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}; duplicates allowed (most common is side=3)
  transform: |
    cycle each square's side by -2 keeping center fixed:
      9->7, 7->5, 5->3, 3->1, 1->9
  constraints:
    - all output squares (after transform) must fit within the grid
    - all output squares must not overlap (min pairwise bbox gap >= 1)
    - all input squares must not overlap
    - 1x1 pixel center needs margin >= 4 from every edge (expands to 9x9)
    - squares may touch grid edges in the input [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first, then invert
  steps:
    1. Pick num_squares in [3, 5] and assign each a distinct non-bg color
    2. Choose output side lengths from {1, 3, 5, 7, 9} (at most one 9x9)
    3. Place output squares on a 16x16 grid (bg=7) with no overlaps and
       all within bounds; use greedy or rejection placement
    4. Invert the transform to get input sides (1->3, 3->5, 5->7, 7->9, 9->1)
    5. Verify each input square also fits in 16x16 centered at the same point;
       reject and re-place if not
    6. Render input grid by painting each square at its input side length
  notes:
    - placing output first guarantees non-overlap of the more constrained state
    - the 9x9 output square dominates space; place it first
    - avoid placing a 9x9 output square whose center has margin < 0 for
      the inverted 1x1 (trivially satisfied) — the real constraint is forward
```
